Triumph of right-wing populists in Austria, in total they come to 29 percent
According to the first preliminary results, a noticeable weakening of both parties of the previous grand coalition and a significant strengthening of the right-wing populist parties brought the early parliamentary elections on Sunday in Austria.
The Austrian People's Party (ÖVP) suffered particularly heavy losses, where the position of the previous chairman Wilhelm Molterer is seriously on the verge.
In contrast, the electoral director of the alliance for the future of Austria (BZÖ), Carinthian state governor Jörg Haider, is gearing up for a triumphant return to federal politics.
According to the previous results, the Social Democracy (SPÖ) remains the strongest force in the country with 29.8 percent of the vote, but compared to the last elections in 2006 it loses 5.5 percent of the vote.
The ÖVP scored even worse, which loses almost nine percent of the votes with 25.6 percent.
These are the worst results of these two major parties in post-war Austrian history, and especially among the People's Party, which had demanded early elections, this is a really catastrophic result.
Even at the beginning of July, when the ÖVP had withdrawn from the coalition, the People's Party had a clear advantage over the SPÖ.
Voters, however, have probably punished the party for letting the government fail.
Already in the first reactions to the results there was speculation about a possible resignation of the party chairman and previous vice-chancellor Wilhelm Molterer.
Observers believe that this could be the case as early as Monday at an extraordinary meeting of the party board.
Such a development would probably pave the way to the most likely way out of the electoral debacle, i.e. a renewal of the grand coalition of SPÖ and ÖVP.
In view of the strengthening of both right-wing populist parties - the liberals (FPÖ) provisionally received 18 percent, the BZÖ eleven percent of the vote - at the same time expressed their apprehension that the year 1999 could be repeated when the People's Party agreed with the populists (at that time the FPÖ was still uniform, in 2005 it had separated itself from the BZÖ) with regard to a joint government, which had even imposed sanctions
Jörg Haider, who has already stated that he is ready to cooperate with each party and assumes a return to Vienna to Austrian federal politics, obviously relies on this development.
In addition, the party of the Greens moved into parliament, which, however, suffered slight losses and slipped from third to fifth place among the Austrian political parties.
No other party has probably mastered the four percent hurdle and therefore did not move into parliament.
Schwarzenberg: Relations with India may cool
If it is not possible to solve the problem with the Czech scientists who have been indicted in India over the unlawful collection of beetles, there could be a certain cooling of Czech-Indian relations, according to Czech Foreign Minister Karel Schwarzenberg.
This was emphasized by the minister in New York after today's meeting with his Indian counterpart Pranab Mukherdshi.
"If this does not enlighten, then for sure, there will be some cooling," he explained after a meeting that took place on a Czech initiative.
Mukherdshi said after the talks that it is difficult for the executive to intervene in judicial matters.
In spite of everything, he stated that in the case of Petr Švácha, who was acquitted by the indictment and received a fine, the central government with the local government in Dardjiling, in whose jurisprudence the matter falls.
"His problem could be clarified," the minister said.
For Emil Kučera, who was sentenced to three years imprisonment, Mukherdshi's view would be the best if he were appealed at a higher authority.
He could thus ask for release on bail and wait in freedom for his verdict, according to the minister.
Schwarzenberg confirmed that his partner had also commented during negotiations.
"He recommends an appeal to a higher court, then a pardon would come into question for him," the head of Czech diplomacy said.
The only way in which the Czech side can intervene in the whole matter is to give the convicted "competent legal support," the Indian minister cited.
At the same time, he said that such help would already be offered to them by the Indian side.
"We have a great interest in maintaining good contacts with India that have a long-term tradition.
The Indians also admit this," Schwarzenberg cited.
At the same time, according to the Czech press agency ČTK in front of his partner, the Czech Republic attached great importance to the problem of Czech entomologists.
"We really think it's a big mistake to imprison and accuse these Czech scientists," he explained.
An answer to the question of whether he had received a commitment from the Indian side to deal with the matter evaded by the minister.
"I've been promised quite a lot," he said.
A Czech judges the wrestling around the bottom of the sea
He approaches an electronic map depicting Australia and the adjacent navular areas.
However, he is not interested in the continent, but a small dot, lost in the blue of the ocean.
"The island belongs to Australia and, together with it, the adjoining seabed.
The area is as large as the continent itself," according to Italian jurist Luigi Santosuosso, who belongs to the thirty-member staff of the U.N
His boss, the division director, the Czech jurist Václav Mikulka, pursues the interpretation and supplements them on and about.
There are workers and enthusiasm here.
So far, the seabed contains a little-known world that is rich in natural sources.
Here you can find petroleum, other ore and other non-renewing raw materials, but also previously unknown living organisms, which, for example, carry the key to the development of a drug against cancer.
A seabed, larger than the mainland
The border states today vehemently registered their claim to the new sea areas.
According to a U.N. Convention, they make use of their rights, on which they agreed in 1982 in Montego Bay in Jamaica.
It is mainly the area of the so-called continental shelf, which often stretches into the sea up to a few hundred kilometers from the shore of a country.
According to the convention, this country has an exclusive right to all sources of the continental shelf.
"Simplified, as a continental shelf the part of the seabed and its soles is considered, which represents a natural continuation of the continent below the sea surface.
It crosses the borders of the shore sea, often also the border of two hundred nautical miles of the so-called exclusive economic zone," explains Mikulka.
The request submitted by the relevant country to the Commission for fixing the external borders of the continental shelf must be evidenced by extensive scientific and technical documentation based on many cost-intensive investigations and measurements.
It is the result of many years of preparatory work.
"One of our tasks is to provide legal and technical technical support to the Commission.
So far, the Commission has negotiated five motions from Russia, Brazil, Australia, Ireland and New Zealand.
Until now, only the latter three countries have had success," Mikulka adds.
An international echo recently met with the success of Australia, which was the first country to significantly increase its naval areas.
However, New Zealand is also a winner - the expansion of the seabed, which was granted to the country last week by the commission, is about 1.6 million square kilometers, about six times more than the size of the actual state.
Both countries have invested millions of dollars in the investigations.
The gain, which in the future flows to them from the use of the seabed, will, as they promise, have an invaluable value.
Other states will submit their applications gradually as well.
They can do this for ten years from the moment they ratify the "Montego Bay agreement."
Where the continental shelf ends, begins the area of the ocean soil, described by the convention as "common heritage of humanity."
There is no longer a state to hold a special position here.
The right to promote in this area is granted by an international institution established by the Convention.
"However, the seabed is not a gold digging land," says Director Mikulka with some exaggeration, adding that "individual areas are legally precisely defined and the system enshrined in the contract."
Czeches in the Pacific
Part of the seabed now also belongs to the Czech Republic.
It shares it with other states of the former Eastern Bloc as part of the joint organization "Interoceanmetal".
Due to the convention in 1991, as well as the investors of a few other states, this received the status of a so-called "rail-breaking investor".
The organization has the right to promote in the ClarionClipperton in the Silent Ocean zone between Mexico, Hawai and Kiribati.
The area to be investigated by the investor is slightly larger than former Czechoslovakia.
The seabed is located there, it is a kind tatra on the seabed.
"You can promote there on an area of a total of 75,000 square kilometers.
The area is a rich site of polymetal concretions that contain nickel, copper, cobalt and manganese," according to another lawyer, the Inder Pakshi Rajan.
