It is reminiscent of a small potato or, with a little imagination, of the fabled Schem who, according to legend, brought the Golem to life.
German special forces were on the scene during hostage liberation
The Egyptian government had requested help from Germany to solve the hostage crisis - the experts came.
When things got serious, the special forces were ready, but they were not used.
More and more details are becoming known about the circumstances of the liberation of eleven European tourists kidnapped in Egypt.
According to the Ministry of Defence and the Ministry of the Interior, German special forces were also indirectly involved in the rescue operation.
Accordingly, both specialists of the Bundeswehr and officials of GSG 9 were the aviator squadron of the Federal Police, the Federal Criminal Police Office and logistics experts of the Technical Relief Agency at the scene of liberation.
According to spokespersons for the ministries on Monday evening, however, the German forces were not needed because the hostage-takers run their prisoners and fled when they recognized signs of a violent liberation.
The Egyptian government has accepted the aid offered by Germany in order to involve it as support staff on the ground.
"Fortunately, this hasn't happened," a spokesman for the Federal Ministry of the Interior said.
Attackers had kidnapped the tour group of the five Germans, five Italians, a Romanian woman as well as her eight Egyptian companions on September 19 in the south of Egypt.
After being kidnapped in a desert area in southern Egypt, the hostages had been deported back and forth in the border area between Egypt, Libya, Sudan and Chad.
The identities of the hostage-takers remained unclear until recently, as well as the details of liberation or possible ransom
Those rescued have meanwhile arrived safely in Cairo.
Steinmeier said the freighted hostages were in the care of the German embassy.
He did not comment on details of the release.
There was initially no information in Berlin about when to return to Germany.
According to "Bild" newspaper, the five holidaymakers and the special forces are to be flown out with an Airbus of the air force and will arrive in Germany on Tuesday.
U.S
The first chamber of the U.S. Congress narrowly failed the 700-billion-dollar bailout for the financial markets.
The Dow Jones rushed into the depths.
By 206 to 227 votes, the U.S. House of Representatives rejected the government's bailout for the financial markets.
Despite all the appeals by U.S. President George W. Bush and leading politicians from both parties, there was no majority in favor of the program, which was intended to contain the chaos in financial markets.
In a dramatic action, the proponents had desperately tried to convince the critics of the 700-billion-dollar package.
Barney Frank, one of Democratic negotiators, had called MPs approval, even if the plan was unpopular.
"It's hard to get political recognition for avoiding something that hasn't happened yet," he conceded.
As recently as the vote, the Dow Jones slumped by more than six percent, or by 600 points.
The reason was concerns of investors, the government bailout could fail.
He recovered shortly before the end of the vote, then crashed again after becoming aware of the failure.
Experts now anticipate further severe turbulence in international financial markets.
Approval of both the House of Representatives and the Senate is required to enter into force of the programme.
Organizers record increasing booking numbers
It seems to become a good winter for the German tour operators:
For travel bookings for the coming months, sales have risen significantly compared to the previous year, according to a study - by even more than 30 percent for the winter season.
Despite weak economic prospects, according to a study, the desire to travel by Germans remains untroubled.
For bookings for the winter season, the travel offices recorded an increase in sales of 31 percent compared to the same period of the previous year, as the German Travel Association (DRV) announced on Monday in Berlin.
According to the study of the market research institute GfK, there are already increased booking figures for the autumn months.
For example, the increase in sales of the travel agencies for October amounted to about eleven percent compared to 2007, the DRV said.
These results are based on figures from GfK, which evaluates monthly booking data from 1200 German travel agencies and extrapolates them for the overall market.
This includes travel bookings until the end of August 2008.
Due to the weak dollar price, travel to the USA in particular is in demand, the DRV said.
Here, the booking increase is around a third.
In addition, Spain, Turkey and Greece as well as the domestic climes are particularly sought-after destinations among the Germans.
In particular, package holidays would be very popular, explained DRV President Klaus Laepple.
In view of rising costs for energy and food, the costs here "are better calculable for the customer than for individually organized holidays".
Consumers could save money, among other things, through early bird discounts or all-inclusive offers, Laepple explained.
Even the summer months were very well booked, so there will be an increase in sales compared to the previous year - although according to another study, the number of Germans who undertook a trip fell compared to the previous year.
