Name of accident victim announced by police
The name of a 63-year-old female pedestrian who died in the hospital after being hit by a vehicle in Essex has been disclosed.
Carole Paulson, residing on Chelmsford Road in Brentwood, was involved in the accident Friday afternoon on Shenfield Road in the centre.
She was taken to Queen's hospital in Romford but later died.
A 51-year-old man has been arrested on suspicion of reckless driving after the accident.
He has since been released on bail.
Turkeya broadens diplomatic horizons
Long before Turkey aspired to join the European Union, European powers were keen to enter Turkey's hinterland.
On the eastern side of the Bosphorus, perhaps just 20 steps within Asia, is one of the finest relics of this failed, imperial endeavor.
Hydarpasha Bahnhof was designed by the two German architects Otto Ritter and Helmut Conu in neo-Renaissance style.
At least that's what the guides tell you.
The building sits rittlings at the end of the platforms with a tower at each corner of the front and thus looks like some provincial German City Hall.
The rocket is filthy.
The whole building disintegrates, for example
But once you are inside the vaulted ticket counter, there is no doubt about the sublimity of the project, whose western access this was.
The station was opened in August 1908 by a German Consortium, the Anatolia-Baghdad Society.
Germany, which was an imperialist-player, had to implement its ambitions to dominate the Middle East by means of a train connection to Damascus, Baghdad, and further after what Saudi Arabia is today.
But Germany's imperialist projects slammed during the first world war.
His railing tracks were attacked by Lawrence of Arabia and his Arabs.
Relative isolation from its surrounding region, generated by the insurmountable borders of the Cold War, has disappeared.
British forces moved out of Egypt and pushed the Turks and their German allies out of Palestine and Syria
And there was also bad news for Hyderpasha station.
In 1917, an ammunition train flew into the air, destroying the ornate roof.
Turkish sources say it was sabotage.
Since then, it has slowly disintegrated, largely transitioned from history.
Nowadays, however, this railway line, at least symbolically, serves as the gateway to Anatolia and everything east of it, in order to highlight the expansion of Turkey's diplomatic horizons.
The ruling Judiciary and Development Party, the AKP, has introduced a completely new foreign policy for the country.
Relative isolation from neighbouring regions, generated by the Cold War's borders, has disappeared.
Now there is a policy of rapprochement.
Remarkably, the Turkish government has good relations with Israel and Syria, the Palestinian splinter groups Hamas and Fatah, Iran and Iraq, and, of course, with the European Union and the United States.
In order to be able to assess the key data of this new foreign policy, I climbed the night train to the Turkish capital Ankara to visit one of the AKP's leading foreign policy experts, Turkish MP Suat Kiniklioglu.
The next morning, about ten and a half hours later, after a bumpy ride on tracks that produced the lockstep of the old world in the well-known documentary "The Night Mail", I arrived in the Turkish capital, which is regarded as a new city, for the new secular state project to turn its back on the Ottoman past.
The AKP, however, known for its Islamist roots, represents a paradigm shift in Turkish unity
This sparked a lot of distrust in the West, especially in Washington.
A taxi has taken me to the AKP's main office, a custom-made, ultra-modern stone and steel structure, in an evolving suburb, surrounded by semi-finished shopping malls and pubs where middle managers met in suits, both men and women, to complete the first deals of the day.
Suat Kiniklioglu could be a centre-right politician from almost every other political party in Europe.
Suit, tie, I'm sure he's even packed a Blackberry.
"The aim of the party's foreign policy is now to re-integrate Turkey with its immediate neighbouring countries.
The Cold War was an exception," he tells me.
Turkey had been cut off from its hinterland.
Nowadays, she has to look east and west at the same time.
There wouldn't be either one or the other.
She'd have to do both.
"But certainly at some point Turkey could," I asked, "be forced to decide?"
"Turkey," he said, "didn't want to face that decision."
He then highlighted a point that almost everyone I was talking to here almost routinely recited: Turkey’s relations, not only to the Middle East, but also to the Caucasus and around the Black Sea, are just as beneficial to Europe and the United States as they are to Turkey.
With Turkey on board, it seems the embassy, Europe as a whole can, figurativelylyly, travel on that same railway snail east towards Asia and the Middle East.
Back at Hydarpasha's station, after another largely sleepless night, I thought of these two German lordships Mr Ritter and Mr. Conu, who had watched the construction of this building 100 years ago.
How surprised they would have been to learn that Europe's ambitions in the Middle East could now, to a large extent, be in Turkey's hands.
And when I waited at the dock for the ferry, which was supposed to bring me back over the Bosphorus, I remembered that in those days Turkey was always called "the sick man of Europe."
At that time at least, there didn’t seem to be much doubt about which continent should have a claim to this fascinating country.
Remembering Vernon Handley and William Woodruff.
Mark Brown writes: As Guildford's musical director since 1962, Vernon "Tod" Handley (obituary on 9/11) was responsible for the Guildford Philharmonic and Choir.
As part of his music program for the city, he founded his great youth choir, Proteus, the following year.
It was intended for teenagers up to the age of 21, but when the chorus sung for the last time in 1979, some of us were already at the end of 20 - we simply refused to leave the choir.
The name "Proteus" (the mythological change of form) stood for the willingness and ability of the choir to tackle any piece of music presented to it, whether it was a madrigal or from the Messiah, whether a capella or with a full orchestra.
The musical versatility of such a young choir was often astonishing, always exciting and consisted only thanks to the inspiration of that man and his enthusiasm for the music, which has never slackened for years.
How often did Death sent us to "let the music speak for itself."
He used to say, "The composer knows what he wants."
It's all in music.
So it sings!"
His way of conducting was absolutely clear and precise, never bossy but also never exuberant.
No matter how high the musical level was, he always demanded an even higher level.
John Frayn Turner writes:
If anyone conductor should have been knighted, it was Vernon Handley.
I've known him well over the past two decades, from 1962 to 1982, during which he led the Guildford Philharmonic.
In the last 40 years I've written reviews for over a hundred of his concerts and I can sincerely say that I've never been disappointed by a single one - often I've been inspired by them.
Death Handley was that rare combination of size and personal modesty.
He loathed conductors who, according to his words, simply "peddled with one or two well-known showhorses in the world."
So many of us will miss him.
William Woodruff
Professor John Beckett writes:
In your obituary for William Woodruff (September 25), you write that "in 1946 he resumed teaching economic history at Oxford and received a Houblon-Norman research fellowship in 1950 ... while at the same time he was studying for a BSc, an MA and a doctoral degree.
This is a conclusion from an author's remark in Woodruff's book "Beyond Nab End," but it's not true.
From 19646 to 1953 he was a lecturer in economic history at the University of Nottingham and during that time he wrote a doctoral thesis on the British rubber industry under the supervision of Professor J.D. Chambers, which was later published in 1958 as "The British Rubber Industry".
He was a 1950-1951 in the United States as part of a Houblon-Norman Scholarship, and spent the summer of 1952 at the Universities of Illinois, Harvard and New York.
In 1953, he left Nottingham and went to Illinois.
It's unknown why Woodruff wanted to cover up that part of his career.
J Vicente Resino at the Faculty of Philology.
`I commend José Vicente Resino `Flamenco und Leben' because his photographs are fantastic in black and white, in documentary style.
They made me aware of them because of their simplicity and because of the force they radiate, how they always deal with respect with the protagonists.
An exhibition that shows a lot of feelings.
Resino knows how to find the magical moments.'
Second lap of excellent sales at Honda.
In November, Honda catches with the marketing of the second-generation Honda Jazz.
While its previous model had a large interior, this open-area sedan offers a much larger one.
In addition, there are the technical intricacies that enable even more convenient driving and gasoline savings.
Changes ahead of the environment revolution.
This is the second generation of Honda Jazz.
Although, aesthetically speaking, the new jazz has a similar line to its predecessor, the changes made in this large limousine are numerous.
Among other things, the innovative design, the new room flexibility, the larger dimensions of the car and the new mechanics will be emphasized.
Still, before starting to analyze the new model, it must be said that six years later, after the first generation had appeared on the market.
According to the initial observations, compared to the other manufacturers, Honda managed to renew this model for a shorter time.
Even though the Honda Jazz is less well-known in Europe, this model is a phenomenon in terms of its sale in the other markets.
That's a case in Japan, in the land where jazz is the best-selling car of the year 2002 and also over the course of 2008.
After jazz was published on the market, about one million models were sold in Japan alone.
The second sales market was the Old Continent, with about half a million cars sold in six years.
Even so, Honda is trying to increase sales of the new jazz model within the segment.
In this way, while Japanese companies sold a total volume of 83 000 models of jazz in 2007, the expected estimates amount to 10 000 cars, which is 17% of the assumed growth in the European market.
As far as the Spanish market is concerned, Honda has sold a total of 8 000 jazz models since 2002, which is 6.7% of the total sale at Honda in Spain.
As far as the new generation of the model is concerned, the managers of the branch in Spain expect to be able to sell 2 000 jazz models per year.
More specifically, the appearance of the new Honad Jazz model makes it possible to cover up the sales reduction in Spain.
In reality, the heads of the brand hope to conclude the year with a total sale of 22 000 models. That’s 4 000 fewer cars than in 2007, a reduction of 16%.
Despite this increase in sales, Honda has increased its market share in Spain compared to the previous year (1.6%) to 1.8%.
At the same time, the Japanese company hopes to be able to reach the 2%
In order to achieve this share, Honda relies on the appearance of a new model.
It is Insight, a hybrid car with 5 doors and a compact size of 4.7 meters in length, the Japanese company hopes to sell about 200 000 models a year all over the world.
According to market estimates by the Japanese manufacturer, 50% of Insight models are sold in the United States, while the remaining share is distributed on the Old Continent and Japan.
The appearance of the insight on the market is scheduled for the first few months of the year, for the first time its public presentation will take place in January in Valencia.
In view of the first generation, the new jazz is growing in all its sizes.
Its length is 3.90 meters (plus 5 centimeters ), its width is 1.69 meters (plus 2 centimeters) and the distance between the axles is 2.50 meters, this leads to the assumption that it has become 5 centimeters taller.
The height, on the other hand, does not change and remains 1.52 meters.
Together with the increase in dimensions, the interior is also enlarged.
This success is due on the one hand to the new space flexibility program and on the other hand, the central position of the fuel container, a solution that was also used in Jazz’s predecessor model.
Also, the capacity of the trunk, which has a system of double loading space, reaches up to 339 liters, i.e. 19 liters greater than the capacity of the trunk of the first generation.
It must be emphasized that these measures are present in the version with a 1.4-litre petrol engine, because the trunk of the Honda Jazz with a 1.2-litre gasoline engine is 379 liters.
Among other improvements, in addition to the additional equipment elements such as leather seats or the possibility to install panoramic windows, improved access to the seats is offered.
The opening angle of the back doors is 80 degrees, with the first jazz having only 67 degrees.
The engines of the second generation of jazz are also new.
At the beginning of its marketing, this small model has 2 versions of the petrol engines: 1.2 and 1.4 liters.
Both variants have i-VTEC system, which enables low fuel consumption and smaller CO2 emissions, has excellent technical performance and at the same time provides high-torque performance for the entire engine life.
The first variant with 1.2 liters achieves the output of 90 hp at 6 000 rotations with a maximum torque of 114 Nm at 4 900 min?1 .
This gasoline engine - Jazz's predecessor model reaches 78 hp - has 5.3 litres of fuel consumption and CO2 emissions of 125 grams.
In view of the previous engine, which reached 83 hp in the earlier generation, it now manages to get to 100 hp.
The Jazz 100 hp model 1.4 has 5.4 liters of gasoline consumption and 128 grams of CO2 emissions.
'car bee' that avoids collisions
In order to promote the development of the technologies for accident prevention, this time Nissan was inspired by nature, more precisely, by the bees.
The Japanese company has developed a robot whose flight goes back to the swarm behavior of bees and avoids accidents.
This robot is a bio-mimetic car robot drive or BR23C and represents the first step in accident prevention technologies.
The engineers from the technical development center of the Nissan Motor Co. have developed this small robot in cooperation with Research Center for Advanced Science and Technology at the renowned University of Tokyo (Japan).
According to Kazuhiro Doi, head of technology PR and R&D brand management, the BR23C represents an extension of the four-layer Nissan safety shield.
This robot is designed to ensure that this brand’s cars are capable of avoiding collisions.
During the flight, each bee builds its own oval-shaped airspace, very similar to the Nissan safety shield.
But the most important are the facet eyes of the bee, which can see over an angle of 300 degrees in all directions, this allows it to fly undisturbed in their personal airspace without interruption.
In order to recreate the all-round perspective, the technicians developed the so-called Laser Range Finder (LRF).
He notices obstacles that are in front of the BR23C at a distance of up to two meters and are within a radius of 180 degrees, calculates the distance to the object and sends a signal to an on-board microprocessor, which is immediately translated into a signal for the avoidance of the collision
`The moment, when obstacle is noticed, the small robot will imitate the bee flight and immediately change direction by turning its wheels 90o or even larger angles to avoid the collision.
The biggest difference from other arbitrary systems is that this evasive manoeuvre takes place absolutely instinctively.
If it weren’t so, the robot might not be able to respond with sufficient speed to avoid obstacles,” explains Toshiyuki Andoh, the director of the Mobility Laboratory and the senior engineer of this project.
In contrast to a bee, the robot cannot deviate upwards or downwards, but also not over-the-courge; it can only move on two dimensions and in the direction that allows it to turn its wheels rather than in the infinite directions and in an infinite way as the bees do.
For this reason, Nissan has used the rotation function in addition to acceleration and braking functions, so that the small robot avoids collisions.
`This device only needs every few seconds to process the information and to act according to this information.
It does not necessary to store the information or the intuitive logic of the manoeuvres made, which is why it can work continuously with only a few dozen KB of storage space.
This sounds surprising, but it’s not about the GBs and not even the MBs of storage space.
This is the first step in the development of the technologies for the immediate prevention of collisions, a technology that Nissan predicts to introduce over the next ten years in the production cars.
The robot works with lithium-polymer batteries that are lighter and last longer than a variety of lithium-ion batteries.
Another possible application of this technology in the future could be the integration of the unit with a GPS navigation system by satellite in order to lead up to the destination while driving, avoiding the obstacles on the way.
She's shaking before she's charged with a mortgage.
