And especially where you can do it, which is also probably the goal of the game.
Surely it has useful information, but without details.
That's more of a side for the teenagers than a place for all viewers.
What will happen when the game is expanded?
Well, when a wonderful room appears in the hotel.
At least something, because it's not about buying a better house
Mangran Stanley provides its capital for MUFG
Morgan Stanley announced the entry of Mitsubishi UFJ Financial Group (MUFG) into its company, which would be the first bank in Japan to spend nine billion dollars and thus take over 21% of its capital, an innovation due to a strong fall in its stock market price.
A week after a provisional settlement had been made public, the two banks declared that MUFG will buy 9.9% of Morgan Stanley common shares at the unified price of 25.25 dollars, resulting in a total amount of three billion dollars.
The assumed price is below 19% of the share's price value as of August 31.
MUFG will invest an additional six billion dollars in acquiring convertible shares, which will secure it a profit of 10% and can be resold at a price of 31.25 dollars.
Morgan Stanley stated that the global business will be made based on a unified average price of 29 dollars, i.e. 28% above the current price value.
Morgan Stanley shares, however, fell 6.79% to 23.07 dollars at 17.00 GMT.
`This strategic connection represents an important opportunity to accelerate Morgan Stanley's transition toward to the status of a holding bank," explained John Mack, director general of the American group.
Morgan Stanley adopted this status last week, which is supposed to allow it to expand its businesses to new areas, but under the supervision of the Federal Reserve.
Their association with MUFG also confirms that the current financial crisis is forcing Wall Street's biggest names to adopt a much more cautious stance than before.
The modalities unveiled on Monday also show that the settlement has been altered over the course of the last few days: last Monday Morgan had simply declared that it would sell up to 20% of its shares to Japan for about 8.5 billion dollars.
`A week ago,time in Tokyo told Toshihide Mizuno, managing director of MUFG
But in view of the current economic and market situation, we wanted to reduce the risks and secure ourselves.”
Based on a balanced average price, MUFG will pay 7% below its price value for its Mangra Stanley shares.
And the business will proceed with a reduction in profit by approximately 20% per share.
The agreement stipulates that MUFG will be able to convert half of its preferred shares into common shares in one year if, within a given period, the latter is exchanged to more than 50% above the sale value, i.e. to approximately 47 dollars.
Morgan Stanley assures that the funds supplied by MUFG will allow him to take advantage of investment opportunities, strengthen their balance sheet and increase their sales.
However, a spokesman for the group refused to provide further details on the strategic projects of the two new allies.
According to MUFG's investment, Morgan Stanley should be able to record a solvency ratio of approximately 15%, one of the best in this sector.
Morgan said his balance sheet had been 'significant' since the end of August.
The last few weeks have been tough for the group, which has had to watch their stock market price decline by half, while the price of insuring against insolvency has skyrocketed.
This price declined on Monday, but remained at a high level, at about 12%.
Goldman Sachs, only other 'survivors' of major investment banks on Wall Street following the bankruptcy of Bear Stearns and Lehman Brothers and the buy-up of Merrill Lynch, proclaimed last week to having withdrawn 15 billion dollars, in part at Berkshire Hathaway.
Rafael Correa in Quito this Monday.
Re-founding the republic around the socialism of the 21st century.
This is the joint project of the Venezuelan Hugo Chávez, the Bolivian Evo Morales and the equatorian Rafael Correa.
It was the third that, following the example of his like-minded and friends, put the plan of a new constitution up for election this Sunday.
Mission fulfilled, because according to 80% of the electoral vote, 64% voted 'yes' in the referendum.
According to these results, 28% voted 'no', 7% of the ballots were invalid and 0.7% were handed empty.
In order for the constitutional project to be passed, one vote has to be scored more than half of all votes cast.
On Sunday, two polls conducted at polling venue exits and a recount by a non-governmental organization had announced a clear victory for `yes', predicting it 63% to 70%.
On the night from Sunday to Monday Rafael Correa had demanded the victory of his constitutional project.
The 'constituency has triumphed in an overwhelming way,' he had insurged in Guayaquil, the seat of the government 280 kilometers southwest of Quito, and congratulated the Equatorians.
Rafael Correa was naturally congratulated by Chávez and Morales, but also by his Chilean like-minded Michelle Bachelet.
The election, held in silence according to the Observation of the Organization of the American States (OAS), referred to a 444-point text to secure the `new socialism' for which the president is campaigning.
The constitutional project transfers state authority to the economy, leaving it with the sole right to 'development planning' in a country that lives largely from the production of oil by foreign companies and from the foreign exchange that emigrators send into the country.
The project, which is pushed forward by Correa elected in 2006, also grants the head-of-state broader power, who will be able to wield up to two consecutive mandates of four years each, and will have an opportunity to dissolve the National Assembly.
Correa already announced that he will make use of this power and seek a new mandate in February 2009.
The constitution also secures the principle of free medical care and education that the president has already begun to introduce in this country of 13.9 million, where 50% of the population lives below the poverty line.
Ultimately, it also envisages prohibiting the presence of foreign troops, which will put an end to the settlement between Quito and Washington on the use of an equatorian base as a starting point for American anti-drug deployments.
The 19 hostages were freed in Chad
An Egyptian command launched the attack on the kidnappers of the eleven tourists and their eight companions who had been captured ten days ago.
They're in Cairo.
The eleven European tourists and their eight Egyptian companions, who were kept in the desert for eleven days, were returned to the Egyptian capital healthy and unharmed this Monday afternoon.
By morning, they had been liberated by an Egyptian command in Chad.
Seemingly in good state, the hostages reached the al-Maza military base right next to Cairo's international airport around 3:30 p.m. aboard a special aircraft.
They were taken directly, without being able to talk to the press, aboard a helicopter to the hospital of Maadi, a foreigner's district of Cairo, in order to be examined there.
They all enjoy `best health' and the kidnappers `no ransom has been paided,' Egyptian Tourism Minister Zoheir Garanna explained on the tarmac.
After being captured on September 19 at an SUV safari at the foot of Mount Ouanat in the border area of Egypt, Sudan and Libya, the group of hostages was repeatedly taken from one hideout to the next by 35 unknown kidnappers.
There are eleven tourists - five Italians, five Germans and one Romanian - and also eight Egyptians: two leaders, four chauffeurs, a border guard and the director of the agency Aegyptus, organizer of the safari.
An Egyptian security official explained that 30 members of the special forces, from board two helicopters, launched the attack at night, just before dawn.
`On both sides, gunshots fell and half of the kidnappers were killed, the rest fled," said this person in charge, who wishes to remain anonymous.
He explained that a total of 150 members of the Egyptian special forces had been sent to Sudan, where they received support from German and Italian commandos.
The head of Italian diplomat Franco Frattini reported from Belgrade, where he is, about the role played by secret agents and elements of Italian special forces.
“We have to acknowledge that we have achieved this result thanks to the professionalism and performance of our men from the secret service and the special forces,” he said.
He also denied that a ransom had been paid.
The previous evening, the Sudanese army had claimed to have stopped an all-terrained vehicle during a patrool, in which the kidnappers were located, six of whom, including the leader from Chad, killed and two arrested.
The army stated that they were connected to a rebel group from Darfour, the Libération du Soudan-Unité (SLA-U) movement, but immediately denied this movement.
Letters - The silent majority of culture
A debate on the financing of culture makes headlines and is the central topic of the current election campaign.
All the better.
But an area of our cultural life remains forgotten.
Who will stand up and denounce the miserable sums afforded to the small museums, the archives, the archaeological excavations or the national historical places that starve miserably?
Which politician will dare to remind the federal government that it neglects its monuments and its culturally significant structures?
That in Quebec, as in Canada, there are no real laws that effectively protect our cultural heritage?
Did you know that the French government provides 3.5 million euros solely for the restoration of Chinon Castle in the Loire Valley?
Among our American neighbors it is written in the constitution of their country that the protection of cultural heritage belongs to the culture of these Americans, and that this cultural heritage must be protected by all citizens, all deputies and all government districts, in mutual coordination.
In Quebec, in the unification of Quebec’s historical societies, at least 40,000 volunteers gathered in 211 historical societies strive to deal intensively with history, save the numerous private archives with a few dimes, and preserve the archaeological remains.
They are also often the watchdogs of our cultural heritage, protesting with the power of despair, when patrons and decision-makers release their bulldozers to our old stones and forgotten cemeteries.
When will there be a stage for history and cultural heritage?
Benelux governments nationalize parts of Fortis Bank
With a partial nationalization, the Benelux countries want to help the Belgian-Dutch Fortis Bank, which has become turbulent due to the U.S. financial crisis: The three states provide a total of EUR 11.2 billion to the banking and insurance institute, which also has branches in Hungary.
The governments of Belgium, Luxembourg and the Netherlands consulted at the highest level over the weekend, together with the President of the European Central Bank and the commissioner of the competition authority of the European Commission.
In the eurozone, Fortis is the first casualty of the international financial market crisis, which has already lasted for a year, so decisions that can now be made in connection with the bank could have a precedent character.
In the spirit of the decision taken on Sunday night, all three states buy the bank's respective shares at about 49 percent.
The bank will presumably sell part of its holdings, above all the insurance activities purchased by ABN Amro last year.
Shares of the Fortis began a steep downturn last week, in the second half of the week there were already serious crisis scenarios looming.
However, analysts agree that the bank, with its 85,000 employees, is far too big for bankruptcy.
The institute's securities fell 12% to a 14-year low on Friday.
Since January, the value of the shares has fallen by more than two-thirds.
In view of the current share prices, the market value of the group amounts to EUR 14 billion, which according to the management is unrealistic.
According to the management, the USD 700 billion expensive American bailout and the resulting uncertainty in the markets is to blame for the downturn in Fortis shares.
On Monday, however, the prices increased slightly.
On Friday, Filip Dierckx was appointed the bank's new director-general.
The financial institution was also looking for a private buyer, but there was no interested party with a factual offer.
The Belgian government, meanwhile, sought reassurance - no investor is supposed to tremble for his money, it said.
There has been no panic among customers so far, the bank's branches have not been stormed.
In Hungary, Fortis has independent branches, but it also mentions leasing companies.
Fouch in the chicken coop
Due to a person’s use of medication, life and health insurance companies often refuse to enter into a contract with a customer.
Data experts fear the data market could become uncontrollable.
"There are data protection programs thating the use of the drug data in any case linked to the consent of the person concerned.
These should be in independent hands, otherwise we entrust the fox to the chicken," quotes the Washington Post Richard Dick, developer of the prescribing data tracking program.
Insurances determine the fees as well as the payout amounts in the event of damage depending on the health status of the customer, and if the risk is assessed too high, the insurance is not taken out at all.
In the past, the customer was sent to a medical examination, and the contract was concluded only in the knowledge of the examination results.
Ten years ago, Dick invented a much cheaper method: Based on the identification number of the customer, he obtains the data from pharmacies for the last one and a half years, i.e. which preparations were purchased, and the program calculates from which disease the customer suffers and what his state of health is.
For example, if he regularly buys large amounts of cholesterol-lowering drugs, it could be concluded that his blood fat levels are untreachably high, which would automatically rank him in a higher risk group.
There are several versions of the program circulating - some even define a risk multiplier, so that the insurances only have to do the paperwork, but there are minor difficulties.
One of the disadvantages is that many drugs can be taken for different purposes, such as migraines and hot flashes, but also against depression.
The latter is a high-risk factor from the point of view of insurers, while those mentioned above are not.
For querying and processing of the data, their own societies have formed which, before they obtain the data, ask the patient for their consent.
In the meantime, they have to do this by law, even though omissions have not yet been punished.
Some companies even process the results of laboratory examinations and associate them with the data on drug use, providing an even more accurate picture of patients’ state of health.
An increasing number of companies have access to the data, and at the same time the control of their use becomes more difficult.
Although there is a separate data protection law for health data, its effect only extends to service providers in the health sector and insurance companies.
The bill has already been submitted to Congress, according to which the authorities could also control the data processing companies.
Dick Richard realizes that this is too little, so he wants to develop his program, so that the data can only be passed on with the consent of the person concerned.
Another question, of course, is that the person concerned can hardly prohibit access to his data, since the insurance companies otherwise do not conclude a contract with him.
And so the chickens are quite somehow left to the fox's grace.
Global meal plan from Marseille
European Union and India doubling trade
Since the European Union and India, on both sides, are each other's leading trading partners, this was the main topic alongside other global problems at the ninth summit.
Today, the French-Indian Summit will also take place, which will mainly discuss cooperation in the nuclear energy sector.
Although the European Union recognizes that India is an emerging, democratic great power that should be given more votes and say in international concerns, the member states have not yet been able to agree on whether or not to support New Delhi's permanent membership in the UN Security Council.
This question was not discussed at the ninth summit in Marseille yesterday.
The French President and current EU Council President Nicolas Sarkozy, as well as Commission President José Manuel Barroso and chief diplomat Javier Solana received Indian Prime Minister Manmohan Singh. An agreement has been reached, according to which trading is to be doubled in the next five years, i.e. increased to a good 100 billion euros.
In view of the fact that the exchange of goods had risen from 25 to 55 billion between 2000 and 2007, this goal is not so ambitious.
In this sense, the 26 existing civil flight agreements with India have also been legally grouped into an agreement, so that in the future air traffic will also be buoyed.
By 2009, India intends to sign the free trade agreement with the Union.
Sarkozy promised to speed up the pact.
The aim is to strike a balance in the currently unbalanced relationship between the EU and India: while the Union is the number one partner in India, New Delhi is only ninth among EU partners.
The summit was dedicated to global concerns.
Among them was the situation in Afghanistan and Pakistan, or terrorism, which both sides strongly condemned. The convening of a UN conference was also urged.
The Doha Round should also be completed, with both sides to blame for the failure.
The EU stressed that it is sticking to its energy and climate programme, i.e. pollutant emissions are to be reduced by at least 20% by 2020.
India also agreed, as well as with the proposal to conclude a kind of global compromise at the upcoming climate summit and to reduce greenhouse gas production by 50 percent by 2050.
The increase in the price of food should also be combated, which is why both support the convening of an international conference on financial crisis.
The Indian Prime Minister visits the French capital today, where he will sign an agreement on the peaceful use of nuclear energy at a bilateral meeting.
Paris, meanwhile, is hoping for orders from French firms worth EUR 20bn for the construction of nuclear reactors in India.
According to agency reports, the agreement is to be decided on 15 years.
Over 300 Italian publishers in Frankfurt
Strong presence of Italian publishers at the International Frankfurt Book Fair.
More than 300 Italian publishers will be present at the 60th book fair, the most important international event for the acquisition of rights.
The international Frankfurt Book Fair expects over 7,000 exhibitors from 101 countries for the current event. They will spread over the 171,790 square meters of the fair and present over 403,000 titles. Turkey will be the guest of honour of the event.
Maria Giro’s under-secretary for the Ministry of Cultural Goods and Cultural Activities will represent the Italian government during the opening ceremony in the exhibition hall: Italy will be represented on the 264 sqm “Punto Italia” exhibition space.
This will be followed by a press conference near the room structure in exhibition hall 5.0 of the book fair: on this occasion, digits, trends and perspectives will be discussed around the publishing industry in Italy.
According to the data, the importance of the books of foreign writers decreases throughout, but especially in the adult and youth division.
Billionaire Steve Fossett's small plane that disappeared a year ago
Steve Fossett's small plane, the billionaire who vanished on September 3, 2007 on a flight over the Nevada desert, was found.
The individual parts have been found on a high plateau of 3000 m altitude, in the area of the Sierra Nevada Mammoth Lakes between the states of California and Nevada.
There were no human remains inside the plane, however.
Madera County Sheriff, John Anderson, completely invalidated the final doubt during a press conference: "The plane has been localized, whereupon a troupe was dispatched. Now we have the certainty that it's the plane that Steve Fossett flew on the day of his disappearance."
Mr. Anderson also further confirmed that no human remains were found on the plane or its proximity.
The 63-year-old billionaire was pronounced dead in February.
The U.S. Office of Road Safety (Ntsb) noted that just a few hours after sighting of the aircraft, a crew was dispatched who identified the plane: it is the Bellanca 8kcab, which was lost on September 3, 2007, after Fossett took off for a 3-hour flight in Yerington, Nevada.
Yesterday, some of Fossett's personal belongings were finally found in California, not far from the Nevada border, by a couple near their home on Mammoth Lakes.
These are two ID cards called Fossetts, both of which had been issued by illinoi's Federal Aviation Administration, cash and a jacket.
The search for Fossett had been abandoned a month after his disappearance.
The billionaire had conquered a place in history through a long episode of records.
In 2002, he succeeded in the sixth attempt, as the first person to circle the world ball in a hot air balloon: a two-week, lone flight.
Three years later, he circumnavigated the world in a single flight without landing with an aircraft.
Under his several records, there is also a treble of 15445 m over the Andes, but with a co-pilot.
Glk, the advertising you don't expect
A suv that falls out of the conventional schemes due to its original design and the special lines, the sweeping surface and its clear angles.
Mercedes-Benz has tried to breathe new breath into the sector with this off-roader.
Precisely because of its extraordinary characteristics, the presentation of the Suv Glk will also be unconventional, which will be shown in the Mercedes-Benz showroom from 17, October.
Thus, the advertising drum, which will accompany the appearance of the new model, will also occur in a new dress, namely in the form of a 3D campaign – a novelty in the car sector in backward Italy.
A project with a maximum effort, which is to be carried out in various chamfers, which the central shopping mile of Milan, the Corso Como, by 15 November.
The first bevel will consist of the presentation of the car in form, whereby 3D glasses, which are distributed in the vicinity of the Corso Como, are intended to give the whole a special look: the new Suv will thus appear as a three-dimensional model in its almost real dimensions.
In fact, the car will seemingly emerge from the poster on which it is pictured.
This is used to illuminate the new Glk in all its details at different moments in order to reassemble itself as a whole.
Undoubtedly it is the concern of Mercedes with this advertising to introduce a new type of car complaint into this rather stereotypical field.
But will it be possible to irritate the interest of future buyers? We will see it.
Now it is clear who the Italian hooligans are
They’re coming out of Triveneto – now they’re risking stadium bans
Who are only these 150-200 boys and girls for whom the whole of Italy is now ashamed? They are part of the "Ultras Italia" group, which has been trailing the Italian national team for years and which, frankly, until recently caused no serious problems.
There have never been clashes with opposing fans so far.
The origin of the group lies in the right milieu.
The sponsors are from Triveneto.
From Verona, Padova, Trieste and Udine.
Among these were other smaller groups from Campania and Puglia in Sofia.
They wear the tricolori, on which the cities of the militants appear: however, these were removed on Saturday to avoid identification.
Their role models are English fans.
In Bulgaria there was now a clash with a truly far-right fan scene, that of Levski.
The Figc had made 144 tickets available to Italian fans for the game: all of them were exhibited by name and thus easy to control.
In fact, they were also all controlled by the Viminal: among those who traveled to Sofia there was no one with an ongoing stadium ban procedure.
Four fans of the Italian team, who had already been burdened in this regard, the ticket and the trip to Bulgaria were also denied.
Now the Viminal is awaiting the reports of the Bulgarian police to take further steps: in this case Tifosi, which have been arrested or identified in the riots, can expect a stadium ban of at least five years.
This is an international ban that prevents access to stadiums across Europe.
So our hooligans won't set foot in a stadium anymore.
Luckily.
But now, of course, there is fear of imitators, who could follow these little more than 150.
"This fire must be escaped immediately, and by no means give such behavior the opportunity for justification," the Viminal said.
That's why, "eyes on especially with a view to the game of the national team on Wednesday in Lecce."
Because, in fact, there is a small core in Puglia of Italian Ultras.
Tomorrow the Osservatorio (sports supervision) will gather on Tuesday the Casms, which can count on a part of the Aise, i.e. the secret services, which deals with the problem of stadium violence.
"Duce-Duce calls and the raised right arm during Mameli's hymn? In Bulgaria, this is not an offense..."
This the idea of Domenico Mazzilli (significantly expressed without joy), who has been the director of the Osservatorio at the Viminal for a few weeks (he took over the post of Francensco Tagliente and Felice Ferlizzi afterwards).
Mazzilli had previously been the police commissioner of Trieste, where a core of the supporters of Ultras Italia, founded in 2020, comes from.
It is true, says Mazzilli, that it is not against the law to praise the Duce in Bulgaria, but undoubtedly the behavior of these 150-200 people was a disgrace.
“I’m not a sociologist—adds the Osservatorio’s No. 1—crimes are punished in the land.
Until now, this group had kept in check, but now it is necessary to reconsider what has happened and will happen.
But we also speak of the whistles during the hymn Mamelis: even this is not a crime, but allow them, because the education is lacking..."
